{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{!-- `sass` compiles a file and an inline block in one call, and the layout
        keeps a head block open for exactly this. --}}
  {{#content "head"}}
  <style>
    {{#sass "5-helpers/styles/board.scss"}}
      $accent: #d9a066;
      .chalkboard .price { color: $accent; }
    {{/sass}}
  </style>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 5 &middot; helpers</p>
    <h1>Six built-in helpers</h1>
    <p>
      kiss registers six Handlebars helpers on every instance. Each one is doing real
      work somewhere on this page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>1. markdown</h3>
      {{#markdown}}
Written as a **block** in the page, straight to HTML:

- no wrapper element needed
- lists, emphasis and links all work
      {{/markdown}}
      <hr>
      <p class="meta">And the same helper reading a string off the model:</p>
      {{markdown model.tastingNotes}}
      <hr>
      {{> "tasting"}}
    </article>

    <article class="card chalkboard">
      <h3>2. sass</h3>
      <p>
        The dark panel and the amber prices below are Sass, compiled during the render
        and written into the page's own <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> tag —
        a file on disk plus an inline block, in a single helper call.
      </p>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Batch brew</td><td class="price">£2.60</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cortado</td><td class="price">£3.10</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h3>3. offset</h3>
      <p class="meta">
        Handlebars counts from zero; <code>offset</code> turns that into a step number.
      </p>
      <ol style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0;">
        {{#each model.steps}}
        <li><strong>Step {{offset @index}}.</strong> {{this}}</li>
        {{/each}}
      </ol>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h3>4. isActive</h3>
      <p class="meta">
        The header nav uses it. Here it is again, spelled out — the same page, four
        different links:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>href</th><th>folderMatch</th><th>on this page</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {{#isActive this href="index.html"}}
          <tr><td><code>index.html</code></td><td>no</td><td>{{#if active}}<span class="tone-good">active</span>{{else}}—{{/if}}</td></tr>
          {{/isActive}}
          {{#isActive this href="/"}}
          <tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>no</td><td>{{#if active}}<span class="tone-good">active</span>{{else}}—{{/if}}</td></tr>
          {{/isActive}}
          {{#isActive this href="brew-guide.html"}}
          <tr><td><code>brew-guide.html</code></td><td>no</td><td>{{#if active}}<span class="tone-good">active</span>{{else}}—{{/if}}</td></tr>
          {{/isActive}}
          {{#isActive this href="" folderMatch=true}}
          <tr><td><code>(site root)</code></td><td>yes</td><td>{{#if active}}<span class="tone-good">active</span>{{else}}—{{/if}}</td></tr>
          {{/isActive}}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h3>5. env</h3>
      {{#env is="dev"}}
      <p class="tone-good">
        <strong>Development.</strong> This build kept its whitespace, wrote a JSON sibling
        beside every page and injected the live-reload script.
      </p>
      {{else}}
      <p class="tone-note">
        <strong>Production.</strong> This build was minified and nothing was injected.
      </p>
      {{/env}}
      {{#env is="prod"}}
      <p class="meta">The production branch of a second <code>env</code> block.</p>
      {{else}}
      <p class="meta">The development branch of a second <code>env</code> block.</p>
      {{/env}}
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h3>6. stringify</h3>
      <p class="meta">Whatever is in scope, printed as JSON. Invaluable when a page is wrong.</p>
      <pre><code>{{stringify model}}</code></pre>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    {{#if model.partials.dynamic}}
    {{> (lookup model.partials 'dynamic')}}
    {{/if}}
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    Six helpers ship with kiss and all six are on this page: <code>markdown</code> renders
    a block, a model string and a <code>.md</code> partial; <code>sass</code> compiles
    <code>styles/board.scss</code> and an inline block into the page head;
    <code>offset</code> numbers the brew steps from one; <code>isActive</code> lights the
    nav and fills the little table above; <code>env</code> switches on
    <code>dev</code>; and <code>stringify</code> dumps the model.
  </p>
  <p>
    This example keeps its own partials folder — the Markdown card and the dynamic
    partial live there — and takes only the layout and the stylesheet from
    <code>_shared</code>. Register your own helpers on <code>kiss.handlebars</code>, which
    is this instance's private Handlebars environment.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
